# EVOLUTION CANDIDATES — <project>

Append-only. **Candidates are evidence, never policy.** Nothing here changes the
Runtime Pack during this project. The pack is read-only for the project's
duration; a dedicated pack-evolution session adjudicates these afterwards, and
every adopted change must carry an acceptance criterion that fails when the
change is removed.

This separation is the whole point. A coordinator that repairs its own governance
mid-project is a coordinator whose governance nobody reviewed, and a pack that
changes under a running project makes that project's evidence uninterpretable.
A pack deficiency found mid-project is recorded here and **worked around with
judgement**; the project is never stopped to perfect the pack.

Ids are `EC-A`, `EC-B`, … Record the ones that cost nothing to work around as
well as the ones that hurt: the cheap ones are how a pattern becomes visible
across projects, and a fourth instance of a small annoyance is a different fact
from a first.

---

## EC-A — <one line: the observation, not the proposed fix>

**Observation.** What happened, described as behaviour rather than as a defect
verdict. Say what the procedure did and what was expected.

**Evidence.** Reproduced how, at which revision, with what output. A candidate
whose evidence is a recollection will not survive the delta audit, which
re-verifies every candidate against pack source rather than accepting its own
description.

**Affected procedure.** The exact file — a skill, a policy file, a template, a
tool.

**Confidence.** High / medium / low, and why. Distinguish "mechanical, reproduced
by running the gate" from "one instance, mechanism plausible".

**Cost/risk.** Of adopting *and* of not adopting. Both, honestly: the risk of
narrowing a safety rule is not the same as the risk of a noisy one.

**Proposed direction.** What might close it — a direction, not a patch. The
evolution pass decides placement (Core enforcement, skill policy, coordinator
judgement, worker profile, or observability only).

**Acceptance criterion if adopted.** The check that **fails when the change is
removed.** Without this a candidate cannot be adopted, because nothing would
later show that it still holds.

